            <ArticleTitle>New mechanisms of glucocorticoid-induced insulin resistance: make no bones about it.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3854-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1172/JCI66180</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">66180</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Glucocorticoids are a powerful tool used to treat a range of human illnesses, including autoimmune diseases and cancer, and to prevent rejection following organ transplantation. While lifesaving for many, they come with a steep price, often leading to obesity, insulin resistance, diabetes, and osteoporosis. In this issue of the JCI, Brennan-Speranza and colleagues provide evidence that the osteoblast-derived peptide osteocalcin is one of the drivers of the metabolic derangements associated with glucocorticoid therapy. This novel mechanism could open up new avenues for the treatment of these disorders.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
